<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagpur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagpur is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, near the geographical centre of the country. Founded around 1702 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakht-buland-shah">Bakht Buland Shah</a> of the Gond kingdom of Deogarh, it became from the 1740s the capital of the Bhonsle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, whose kingdom stretched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. The British annexed it in 1853, and it later served as capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a>; it is known for its oranges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagpur.jpg" alt="Nagpur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kawade Sahil · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deekshabhoomi_Nagpur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nagpur lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a> near the middle of the quadrilateral joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>. The Zero Mile Stone marks the geographical centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and was used by the British to measure distances across the subcontinent. The city takes its name from the Nag River; pur means 'city' in many Indian languages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> held that both were named after the Nags, opponents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-aryans">Indo-Aryans</a>. An earlier name, Fanindrapura, comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> word for a cobra's hood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human settlement around Nagpur goes back to the 8th century BCE. The first reference to the name Nagpur is in a 10th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate inscription from Devali in Wardha District, which records a grant of a village under the Rastrakuta king Krsna III in the Saka year 862 (940 CE).</p><p>The Gond king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakht-buland-shah">Bakht Buland Shah</a> of Deogarh founded the city in 1702 by joining twelve small villages. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chand-sultan">Chand Sultan</a> made Nagpur his capital and turned it into a walled town. After Chand Sultan died in 1739, Wali Shah, an illegitimate son of Bakht Buland, usurped the throne, and Chand Sultan's widow called in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> leader Raghoji Bhosale of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a>. After 1743 a series of Maratha rulers began, starting with Raghoji Bhosale, who had conquered Deogarh, Chanda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> by 1751.</p><p>The British East India Company took Nagpur in the 19th century and made it the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces and Berar</a>. The reorganisation of states in 1956 moved Nagpur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a>, and Nagpur became the second capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> under the Nagpur Pact, by which one session of the state legislature is held there. On 14 October 1956 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> and his supporters converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> there, which started the Dalit Buddhist movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nagpur is a major commercial centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidarbha">Vidarbha</a> region and is known for its oranges, which have earned it the name Orange City. It has its own bench of the Bombay High Court, established in 1936. Deekshabhoomi, where the 1956 conversion took place, is a Buddhist pilgrimage site with the largest hollow stupa in the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagpur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Chhatrapati at Satara to the Peshwa's administration at Pune, which supervised a confederacy of semi-autonomous chiefs, among them the Gaekwads of Baroda, Holkars of Indore, Scindias of Gwalior and Bhonsles of <b>Nagpur</b>, who collected revenue and maintained armies while acknowledging the Peshwa's leadership and the Chhatrapati's formal sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…in the 19th century; in 1956 B. R. Ambedkar, the jurist and campaigner for the rights of the Dalits, became a Buddhist at <b>Nagpur</b> with hundreds of thousands of followers; and in the 20th century Buddhist teachers and Asian migrants established communities in Europe, North America and Australia.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Mahurjhari, near <b>Nagpur</b>, became a major centre of bead manufacturing in the early historic period.</blockquote><a href="/culture/south-indian-megalithic-culture">South Indian megalithic culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Savarkar, an atheist, opposed the caste system: under him the Hindu Mahasabha held all-caste dinners in <b>Nagpur</b> and Kanpur, and he gave parents money so that their children of the so-called lower castes would attend school.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century the Bhonsle Marathas of <b>Nagpur</b> extended their power over the region, and Chanda was annexed around 1751.</blockquote><a href="/state/chanda-kingdom">Chanda Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>He founded the city of <b>Nagpur</b> around 1702, which became the capital under his son Chand Sultan.</blockquote><a href="/state/deogarh-kingdom">Deogarh Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
